\section*{\textcolor{plakshamagenta}{Abstract}}

This project presents a comparative mathematical analysis of two rocket
nose fairing designs to investigate how changes in internal
geometry affect structural mass. Both designs use the same
fixed external tangent-ogive profile but differ in their internal
profiles. The baseline design uses an inner radius equal to
90\% of the outer radius, while the modified design uses a
constrained cubic polynomial.

The fairing is modelled as a hollow surface of revolution.
Definite integration is used to calculate the internal and
material volumes of both designs. The material volumes are
then converted into estimated masses using the assumed
density of aluminium 6061. The modified profile is also
checked against the specified internal-volume and minimum
wall-thickness requirements.

The project further develops a simplified model of the
rocket's vertical powered ascent using first-order ordinary
differential equations. Euler's method is used to estimate
changes in altitude, velocity and mass under the assumed
propulsion conditions.

The modified profile gives an estimated material-mass reduction of 48.09\%
(332.38\,kg to 172.52\,kg) with a larger internal volume, and meets the
0.020\,m wall-thickness requirement over $0.4\le x\le1.5$\,m. However, the
calculations rest on idealised geometric and physical assumptions and do not
constitute a structural safety assessment or a validated prediction of
rocket performance.


\vspace{0.5cm}

\noindent
\textbf{Keywords:} 
 Rocket nose fairing, tangent-ogive, cubic polynomial, surface of revolution, volume of revolution, ordinary differential equations, Euler's method.

