% 1. INTRODUCTION
% ==================================================
\section{\textcolor{plakshamagenta}{Introduction}}

A rocket nose fairing is a protective structure fitted at the front of a launch vehicle. It protects the payload from aerodynamic forces, heating and the external environment during flight. However, the fairing also contributes to the total mass of the rocket. Since the rocket must accelerate its own mass during launch, reducing the mass of the fairing could leave more of the vehicle's mass capacity available for the payload or other requirements.

This project investigates whether the structural mass of a rocket nose fairing can be reduced by modifying its internal geometry while keeping its external shape fixed. The aim is to compare two fairing designs and estimate how changing the inner profile affects the internal volume, material volume and resulting structural mass.

The fairing is modelled as a hollow surface of revolution. Its external profile is represented by a fixed tangent-ogive curve. Two internal profiles are considered: a baseline profile whose radius is 90\% of the outer radius, and a modified profile represented by a constrained cubic polynomial. Since the outer profile remains unchanged, the comparison focuses on the effect of the internal geometry on the amount of material required.

Mathematical functions are used to describe the inner and
outer profiles. Derivatives are used to examine the smoothness
of the outer profile at the junction between the nose and the
cylindrical body. Definite integration is used to calculate
the internal volume and material volume of both designs.
The material volume is then converted into an estimated
mass using the density of aluminium 6061.

The modified design is assessed against two geometric requirements: its internal volume must be at least as large as that of the baseline design, and its wall thickness must satisfy the specified minimum over the evaluation interval. These conditions allow the two designs to be compared without changing the external profile.

The project also investigates how the estimated fairing mass affects a simplified model of the rocket's vertical powered ascent. A system of ordinary differential equations is developed to describe altitude, velocity and mass under the effects of thrust, gravity and aerodynamic drag. Euler's method is used to obtain numerical estimates of the rocket's motion. The same engine and propellant assumptions are used for both designs so that the effect of the change in fairing mass can be examined.

The analysis is based on mathematical and numerical models. The minimum wall thickness is treated as a geometric constraint and does not establish that the fairing is structurally safe under actual launch conditions. 
