\section{\textcolor{plakshamagenta}{Mathematical Background}}

\subsection{Fairing Geometry and Continuity}\label{sec:geometry}

This section sets up the shape of the nose fairing and checks how smoothly it joins the cylindrical body of the rocket.

\subsection{Surface modelling}\label{sec:surface}

We assume the fairing is axisymmetric, so one profile $y=f(x)$ rotated about the $x$-axis describes the whole surface. We put the origin at the tip of the nose. The $x$-axis runs along the rocket towards the tail, and $y\ge 0$ is the distance from the axis. The nose runs from $x=0$ to $x=L$, and the cylindrical body of radius $R$ starts at $x=L$. Rotating the profile about the $x$-axis gives the surface
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{X}(x,\theta)=\bigl(x,\; y(x)\cos\theta,\; y(x)\sin\theta\bigr),
  \qquad (x,\theta)\in D=[0,L]\times[0,2\pi].
  \label{eq:surface}
\end{equation}
Every slice across the nose ($x=$ constant) is a circle of radius $y(x)$. The body is built the same way, with $y(x)=R$ for $x\ge L$.

The first part of $\mathbf{X}$ is just $x$, so $\mathbf{X}_x=(1,\;y'\cos\theta,\;y'\sin\theta)$ is never zero. That means $x$ works as a parameter on both sides of the join. The angle $\theta$ only shows up inside $\cos\theta$ and $\sin\theta$, and these can be differentiated as many times as we like. So the surface is exactly as smooth as the profile $y(x)$, and any question about how smooth the fairing is becomes a question about $y,y',y''$, so we analyse the profile (Section~\ref{sec:continuity}). Note that $\mathbf{X}_\theta=(0,-y\sin\theta,y\cos\theta)$ vanishes at the tip $y=0$, so the parametrisation is singular there.

\subsection{Geometry of the outer profile}\label{sec:outer}

Table~\ref{tab:params} lists the values we use in every example. The radius grows from $y(0)=0$ at the tip to $y(L)=R$ at the join.

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Geometric parameters of the fairing.}\label{tab:params}
\begin{tabular}{|c|l|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Symbol} & \textbf{Meaning} & \textbf{Value}\\ \hline
$R$ & radius of the cylindrical body (diameter $2R=1.00$\,m) & $0.50$\,m\\
$L$ & length of the nose, from tip to join & $1.50$\,m\\
$\rho$ & radius of the ogive arc, derived in \eqref{eq:rho} & $2.50$\,m\\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The outer profile is a tangent ogive, the classic bullet-shaped rocket nose \cite{nasa1973}. It is an arc of a large circle of radius $\rho$, whose centre lies directly below the join. The part of the circle from the tip up to the body is the top half of the nose profile. The ogive is \emph{tangent} to the cylinder because the arc and the line $y=R$ have the same slope at $x=L$, so the join has no corner.

Because the profile is part of a circle, it bends by the same amount everywhere: its curvature is $1/\rho$. At the join it matches the height and slope of the body. But the body is straight, with curvature $0$, while the arc still has curvature $1/\rho$. We check this in Section~\ref{sec:continuity}.

For the arc to touch the line $y=R$ at $x=L$, its centre must be at $(L,\,R-\rho)$. The arc must also pass through the tip $(0,0)$:
\[
  (0-L)^2+\bigl(0-(R-\rho)\bigr)^2=\rho^2
  \;\Longrightarrow\; L^2+R^2-2R\rho=0 .
\]
Solving for $\rho$ gives the radius of the ogive. The top half of the circle is the profile (this needs $L\ge R$, which is true here):
\begin{equation}
  \rho=\frac{R^2+L^2}{2R},\qquad
  y_o(x)=R-\rho+\sqrt{\rho^{2}-(L-x)^{2}},\quad 0\le x\le L.
  \label{eq:rho}
\end{equation}
With $R=0.5$\,m and $L=1.5$\,m, we get $\rho=(0.25+2.25)/1.0=2.5$\,m, and the centre is at $(1.5,\,-2.0)$. Figure~\ref{fig:profile} shows the profile and Figure~\ref{fig:surface} the surface obtained from \eqref{eq:surface}.

