\subsection{Continuity}\label{sec:continuity}

\subsubsection{Meaning of $C^0$, $C^1$ and $C^2$}

A function is of class $C^k$ if it and its first $k$ derivatives are all continuous. Two pieces of a surface that meet along an edge are $C^0$ there if they meet with no gap, and $C^k$ if their first $k$ derivatives also match there \cite{farin2002}. For the fairing this is a check on the profile at $x=L$. $C^0$ means there is no step in the skin, $C^1$ means there is no corner, and $C^2$ means the bending (curvature) does not jump. For a graph $y=f(x)$ the curvature is \cite{strang2016}
\begin{equation}
  \kappa(x)=\frac{y''(x)}{\bigl(1+y'(x)^2\bigr)^{3/2}},
  \label{eq:kappa}
\end{equation}
with $\kappa<0$ when the profile bends towards the axis. At the join $y'=0$, so there $\kappa=y''$.

\subsubsection{Derivatives of the ogive}

Let $u=\rho^2-(L-x)^2$. Then $y_o=R-\rho+\sqrt u$ and $u'=2(L-x)$. The chain rule gives
\begin{equation}
  y_o'=\frac{u'}{2\sqrt u}=\frac{L-x}{\sqrt{\rho^{2}-(L-x)^{2}}}.
  \label{eq:dyo}
\end{equation}
For the second derivative, we use the product rule on $(L-x)\,u^{-1/2}$:
\[
  y_o''=-u^{-1/2}-\tfrac12 (L-x)\,u^{-3/2}\,u'
       =-\frac{u+(L-x)^2}{u^{3/2}},
\]
and since $u+(L-x)^2=\rho^2$,
\begin{equation}
  y_o''=-\frac{\rho^{2}}{\bigl(\rho^{2}-(L-x)^{2}\bigr)^{3/2}}.
  \label{eq:d2yo}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Continuity at the join}

For $x\ge L$ the body has $y_b=R$, $y_b'=0$ and $y_b''=0$. Writing $y(L^-)$ for the value just before the join:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $C^0$: from \eqref{eq:rho}, $y_o(L)=R-\rho+\sqrt{\rho^2}=R$, so the ogive is $C^0$.
  \item $C^1$: from \eqref{eq:dyo}, $y_o'(L^-)=0/\rho=0$, so the ogive is $C^1$.
  \item $C^2$: from \eqref{eq:d2yo}, $y_o''(L^-)=-\rho^2/(\rho^2)^{3/2}=-1/\rho=-0.400$\,m$^{-1}\ne 0$, so the ogive is \emph{not} $C^2$.
\end{itemize}
The curvature of the ogive is constant along the whole nose. Since $1+y_o'^2=\rho^2/u$, equation \eqref{eq:kappa} gives
\[
  \kappa_o(x)=\frac{-\rho^2u^{-3/2}}{(\rho^2/u)^{3/2}}=-\frac1\rho=-0.400\ \mathrm{m^{-1}}\qquad(0<x\le L),
\]
as expected for a circle. On the body $\kappa=0$, so at the join the curvature jumps from $-0.400$\,m$^{-1}$ to $0$ (Figure~\ref{fig:derivs}).

\begin{figure}[h!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figs/fig3_ogive_derivatives.pdf}
\caption{Slope, second derivative and curvature of the tangent ogive, followed by the body. The open circle is the nose-side value at $x=L$ and the filled circle the body value. The slope is continuous; $y''$ and $\kappa$ jump by $1/\rho=0.400$\,m$^{-1}$.}\label{fig:derivs}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Inner cubic at the join.}
The modified inner profile of Section~\ref{sec:fairing-compare} can be written
$y_{i,c}=0.48\,\xi^2(3-2\xi)+C\,\xi(1-\xi)^2$ with $\xi=x/L$ and
$C\approx0.98181$. It satisfies $y_{i,c}'(L)=0$ and
$y_{i,c}''(L)=(2C-2.88)/L^2\approx-0.407\ \mathrm{m^{-1}}$, while
$y_o''(L)=-0.400\ \mathrm{m^{-1}}$. Hence $t=y_o-y_{i,c}$ satisfies
$t'(L)=0$ and $t''(L)\approx+0.007\ \mathrm{m^{-1}}$, so the wall thickness
has a shallow local minimum of $0.020$\,m at the join, which is why its
margin there is small.

