\section{\textcolor{plakshamagenta}{Mathematical Analysis}}

\
\subsection{Forces Acting on the Rocket}

During vertical powered ascent, three main forces act on the
rocket: thrust, gravity and aerodynamic drag. Thrust acts
upwards, while gravity and drag act downwards.

The gravitational force is

\begin{equation}
F_g(t)=m(t)g(h(t)).
\end{equation}

The aerodynamic drag is

\begin{equation}
D(t)=\frac{1}{2}\rho_{\mathrm{USSA76}}(h(t))
C_D A v(t)^2.
\end{equation}
where $\rho_{\mathrm{USSA76}}(h)$ denotes the atmospheric
density at geometric altitude $h$, calculated using the
U.S. Standard Atmosphere 1976 layer equations. Geometric
altitude is converted to geopotential altitude for the
density calculation. The model is applied up to 84.852 km
geopotential altitude, above which density is set to zero
as a simplifying assumption. The drag coefficient is
$C_D = 0.3$ [6] and the reference frontal area is
$A = \pi(0.5)^2 = 0.785398$ m$^2$.

% --------------------------------------------------
% 4.2 Equation of Motion
% --------------------------------------------------


\subsection{Equation of Motion}

Taking upward as the positive direction, Newton's second law
gives

\begin{equation}
T(t)-D(t)-m(t)g(h(t))
=m(t)\frac{dv}{dt}.
\end{equation}

Therefore, the acceleration is

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\frac{dv}{dt}=
\frac{T(t)-D(t)-m(t)g(h(t))}{m(t)}
}
\end{equation}
\subsection{Variable Mass and Propellant Consumption}
Since velocity is the rate of change of altitude,

\begin{equation}
\boxed{\frac{dh}{dt}=v(t).}
\end{equation}

During powered ascent, the rocket continuously consumes
propellant. Assuming a constant propellant mass-flow
rate $\dot{m}$, the rate of change of rocket mass is

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\frac{dm}{dt}=-\dot{m}.
}
\end{equation}

The negative sign indicates that the total rocket mass
decreases as propellant is consumed.

Combining the equations for altitude, velocity and mass,
the complete first-order system is

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\frac{dh}{dt} &= v,\\[4pt]
\frac{dv}{dt} &=
\frac{T-D-mg(h)}{m},\\[4pt]
\frac{dm}{dt} &= -\dot{m}.
\end{aligned}
}
\label{eq:rocket_system}
\end{equation}

This coupled system describes the rocket's vertical
powered ascent under the effects of thrust, gravity,
aerodynamic drag and propellant consumption.

