\subsection{Double Integrals: Fairing Surface Area}
The fairing is modelled as a surface of revolution about the rocket's longitudinal axis. Let $x$ denote axial distance from the nose tip and $y(x)$ the radial profile. For a surface generated by revolving $y(x)$ through $2\pi$, the area element is $dS=2\pi y\,ds$, where $ds=\sqrt{1+[y'(x)]^2}\,dx$. Hence
\begin{equation}\label{eq:area-general}
A=2\pi\int_0^L y(x)\sqrt{1+[y'(x)]^2}\,dx.
\end{equation}
This is the surface-area form of a double integral: the azimuthal integration contributes $2\pi$, while the meridional arc-length element accounts for the profile slope. All profiles share $R=0.5\,\mathrm{m}$ and $L=1.5\,\mathrm{m}$. The tangent-ogive generating-circle radius is $\rho=(R^2+L^2)/(2R)=2.5\,\mathrm{m}$.


The parameterisation $\mathbf X(x,\theta)=(x,y(x)\cos\theta,y(x)\sin\theta)$ gives
$\|\mathbf X_x\times\mathbf X_\theta\|=y(x)\sqrt{1+[y'(x)]^2}$.
Thus the double integral over $x$ and $\theta$ reduces to (1). The slant factor
$\sqrt{1+[y']^2}$ is essential: using the axial increment $dx$ alone would
underestimate the true curved surface area.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.47\linewidth]{Fig_4_3_2_Surface_Revolution.png}\hfill
\includegraphics[width=0.43\linewidth]{Fig_4_3_3_Differential_Surface_Element.png}
\caption{Surface-of-revolution parameterisation and differential area element.}
\label{fig:surface-element}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Tangent-ogive profile} The radial profile and derivative are
\begin{equation}
y_o(x)=\sqrt{6.25-(1.5-x)^2}-2,\qquad
y'_o(x)=\frac{1.5-x}{\sqrt{6.25-(1.5-x)^2}}.
\end{equation}
They satisfy $y_o(0)=0$ and $y_o(1.5)=0.5\,\mathrm{m}$. Substitution in (\ref{eq:area-general}) gives
\begin{align}
A_o&=2\pi\int_0^{1.5}\left[\sqrt{6.25-(1.5-x)^2}-2\right]
\sqrt{1+\frac{(1.5-x)^2}{6.25-(1.5-x)^2}}\,dx\\
&=5\pi\int_0^{1.5}\left[1-\frac{2}{\sqrt{6.25-(1.5-x)^2}}\right]dx
\approx \boxed{3.3458\,\mathrm{m^2}}.
\end{align}
The simplification uses $1+(1.5-x)^2/[6.25-(1.5-x)^2]=6.25/[6.25-(1.5-x)^2]$.

\subsubsection{Surface areas of the two designs}
Applying (\ref{eq:area-general}) to the outer ogive and to the two inner
profiles of Section~\ref{sec:fairing-compare} (evaluated numerically) gives
\begin{table}[H]\centering\small
\caption{Surface areas of the outer and inner profiles over $0\le x\le1.5$\,m.}
\begin{tabular}{lc}\toprule
Surface & Area ($\mathrm{m^2}$)\\\midrule
Outer ogive $y_o$ & 3.3458\\
Baseline inner $0.9y_o$ & 2.9889\\
Modified inner cubic $y_{i,c}$ & 3.1523\\\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
The outer area is shared by both designs. The cubic inner surface is
$0.1634\,\mathrm{m^2}$ larger than the baseline inner surface because its
inner radius is larger over most of the nose (a thinner wall); the same
larger radius gives the larger internal volume. Area alone does not determine
mass: the material volume
$V_{\rm material}=\pi\int_0^L(y_o^2-y_i^2)\,dx$ of
Section~\ref{sec:fairing-compare} does. For a uniform skin of thickness $t$,
$m\approx\rho_m tA$ is only a first-order check; joints, stiffeners and
thermal protection are not included.

\subsection{Triple Integrals: Fuel-Tank Volume and Mass}
The LOX oxidiser and RP-1 fuel are stored in separate tanks. The adopted design inputs are total propellant mass $m_p=4330\,\mathrm{kg}$, oxidiser-to-fuel mass ratio $m_{\rm LOX}/m_{\rm RP1}=2.7$, densities $\rho_{\rm LOX}=1142\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ and $\rho_{\rm RP1}=810\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$. Each tank comprises a cylinder of radius $r=0.45\,\mathrm{m}$ and length $\ell$, joined to two half-ellipsoidal caps; the caps together form one ellipsoid with axial semi-axis $c=0.30\,\mathrm{m}$.

\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.63\linewidth]{Fig_4_4_1_Tank_Geometry.png}
\caption{Idealised separate propellant tanks: cylindrical barrels with ellipsoidal end caps.}\label{fig:tanks}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Propellant mass and liquid-volume allocation} From $m_p=m_{\rm LOX}+m_{\rm RP1}$ and $m_{\rm LOX}=2.7m_{\rm RP1}$,
\begin{equation}
m_{\rm RP1}=\frac{4330}{3.7}=1170.27\,\mathrm{kg},\qquad
m_{\rm LOX}=3159.73\,\mathrm{kg}.
\end{equation}
The required liquid volumes follow from $V=m/\rho$:
\begin{equation}
V_{\rm LOX}=\frac{3159.73}{1142}=2.76684\,\mathrm{m^3},\qquad
V_{\rm RP1}=\frac{1170.27}{810}=1.44478\,\mathrm{m^3}.
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Triple-integral tank geometry} In cylindrical coordinates $(s,\theta,z)$, the Jacobian is $s$. The cylindrical barrel volume is therefore
\begin{equation}
V_{\rm cyl}=\int_0^\ell\!\int_0^{2\pi}\!\int_0^r s\,ds\,d\theta\,dz
=\pi r^2\ell.
\end{equation}
For the joined ellipsoidal caps, the radial boundary is $s=r\sqrt{1-x^2/c^2}$ for $-c\leq x\leq c$. Thus
\begin{align}
V_{\rm caps}&=\int_{-c}^{c}\!\int_0^{2\pi}\!\int_0^{r\sqrt{1-x^2/c^2}}s\,ds\,d\theta\,dx\\
&=\pi r^2\int_{-c}^{c}\left(1-\frac{x^2}{c^2}\right)dx
=\frac{4}{3}\pi r^2c.
\end{align}
The Jacobian factor $s$ in cylindrical coordinates is essential because the
circumference of each cylindrical shell increases with radius. The cap integral
uses the ellipsoidal radial boundary and gives the volume of both half-caps together.
