capacity. Raising the ullage fraction $u$ in
$V_{\rm capacity}=V_{\rm liquid}/(1-u)$ lengthens the barrel. All terms in
$V_{\rm tank}$ have units of $\mathrm{m^3}$, and the positive barrel lengths
show the cap geometry leaves a feasible cylindrical section. These are ideal
internal dimensions; wall thickness, insulation, supports and pressurisation
are excluded \cite{flaherty,nist}.

\subsection{Line Integrals: Work Done by Forces}
Work by a force field along a path $C$ is $W=\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r$. For the model's one-dimensional, upward powered ascent, $\mathbf r(t)=h(t)\hat{\mathbf k}$ and $d\mathbf r=v(t)\hat{\mathbf k}\,dt$, from $t=0$ to burnout $t_b$. The instantaneous rocket mass is $m(t)$ and gravity varies with altitude as
\begin{equation}
g(h)=g_0\left(\frac{R_E}{R_E+h}\right)^2,
\quad g_0=9.80665\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}},\quad R_E=6.371\times10^6\,\mathrm{m}.
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{ Gravitational work} Since gravity acts downward, $\mathbf F_g=-m(t)g(h(t))\hat{\mathbf k}$, and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:wg}
W_g=\int_0^{t_b}\mathbf F_g\cdot\dot{\mathbf r}\,dt
=-\int_0^{t_b}m(t)g(h(t))v(t)\,dt.
\end{equation}
\subsubsection{Aerodynamic-drag work} For upward velocity, drag is also downward. Its magnitude is modelled as $D(t)=\frac12\rho_a(h(t))C_dAv(t)^2$, with baseline $C_d=0.3$ and frontal reference area $A=\pi(0.5)^2=0.785398\,\mathrm{m^2}$. Hence
\begin{equation}\label{eq:wd}
W_D=-\int_0^{t_b}D(t)v(t)\,dt.
\end{equation}
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.43\linewidth]{Fig_4_5_1_Rocket_Free_Body.png}\hfill
\includegraphics[width=0.43\linewidth]{Fig_4_5_2_Line_Integral_Path.png}
\caption{Forces during vertical ascent and the path over which work is evaluated.}
\label{fig:work-path}
\end{figure}
The negative signs in (\ref{eq:wg})--(\ref{eq:wd}) follow directly from the dot product: both forces oppose the upward displacement.

The trajectory is computed with the baseline model of Section~\ref{sec:euler} using explicit Euler integration at $\Delta t=0.01\,\mathrm{s}$, with the final step shortened to reach propellant depletion. The line integrals are evaluated on that same trajectory. With $g_i=g(h_i)$ and $D_i=\frac12\rho_a(h_i)C_dAv_i^2$, the trapezoidal sums in altitude are
\begin{equation}
W_g\approx-\sum_i\frac{m_ig_i+m_{i+1}g_{i+1}}2\,(h_{i+1}-h_i),\quad
W_D\approx-\sum_i\frac{D_i+D_{i+1}}2\,(h_{i+1}-h_i).
\end{equation}
The substitution $dh=v\,dt$ converts the time integrals into force--displacement
integrals. At each pair of adjacent trajectory points, the force is averaged at
the endpoints and multiplied by the altitude increment. The same Euler trajectory
is used for both work terms, ensuring that their numerical estimates correspond to
the same ascent history. The final shortened integration step ensures that the
mass reaches the prescribed burnout value rather than overshooting it.
