% 4.6 Numerical Solution Using Euler's Method
% --------------------------------------------------

\subsection{Numerical Solution Using Euler's Method}
\label{sec:euler}
The coupled differential equations do not have a simple analytical solution because the acceleration depends on changing mass, altitude, atmospheric density and velocity. Therefore, a numerical method is used to approximate the rocket's motion over small time intervals.
Euler's method approximates the value of a variable at the next time step using its current value and rate of change.
Let the time step be \(\Delta t\), and let the values at the \(n\)-th time step be \(h_n\), \(v_n\) and \(m_n\).

The altitude is updated using
\[
\boxed{
h_{n+1}=h_n+v_n\Delta t
}
\]
The acceleration at each time step is calculated from the force equation:
\[
a_n=
\frac{T-D_n-m_ng(h_n)}{m_n}.
\]
The velocity is then updated using
\[
\boxed{
v_{n+1}=v_n+a_n\Delta t
}
\]
The mass is updated using the constant mass-flow rate:
\[
\boxed{
m_{n+1}=m_n-\dot{m}\Delta t
}
\]
All runs use $\Delta t=0.01$\,s, $T=100$\,kN, $I_{sp}=305$\,s,
$\dot m=33.4333$\,kg/s and the atmosphere of Section~4.1, starting from
$h_0=v_0=0$ and $m_0=m_{\rm dry}+4330$\,kg. At each step density, drag and
gravity are recomputed from the current state, and the loop runs until the
propellant is exhausted, with the last step shortened so the mass stops
exactly at $m_{\rm dry}$. The numerical results are summarised in
Table 7.

% --------------------------------------------------
% 4.7 Material Volume and Structural Mass of the Fairing
% --------------------------------------------------


\subsection{Comparison of Baseline and Modified Fairing Geometry}
\label{sec:fairing-compare}
The objective is to reduce the material mass of the fairing while
maintaining the same external geometry. Both designs use a fixed
tangent-ogive outer profile, but differ in their internal profiles.
The comparison is based on internal volume, material volume,
estimated mass and minimum wall thickness.

\subsubsection{Outer and Inner Profiles}

The outer profile is represented by the tangent-ogive equation

\begin{equation}
y_o(x)=R-\rho+\sqrt{\rho^2-(L-x)^2},
\end{equation}

where $R=0.50\,\mathrm{m}$, $L=1.50\,\mathrm{m}$ and
\[
\rho=\frac{R^2+L^2}{2R}=2.50\,\mathrm{m}.
\]

The baseline design uses an inner radius equal to 90\% of the
outer radius:

\begin{equation}
y_{i,b}(x)=0.9y_o(x).
\end{equation}

For the modified design, the inner profile is represented by
a cubic polynomial. Let

\begin{equation}
y_{i,c}(x)=Au^3+Bu^2+Cu,
\qquad u=\frac{x}{L}.
\end{equation}

We impose the boundary conditions

\begin{equation}
y_{i,c}(0)=0,\qquad
y_{i,c}(L)=0.48,\qquad
y'_{i,c}(L)=0.
\end{equation}

These conditions ensure that the profile closes at the tip,
has an inner radius of $0.48\,\mathrm{m}$ at the base, and
has a horizontal tangent at the base.

Applying the conditions gives

\begin{align}
A+B+C&=0.48,\\
3A+2B+C&=0.
\end{align}

Solving in terms of the free coefficient $C$,

\begin{equation}
A=C-0.96,\qquad B=1.44-2C.
\end{equation}

The remaining coefficient is selected using the wall-thickness
condition at $x=0.4\,\mathrm{m}$:

\begin{equation}
y_o(0.4)-y_{i,c}(0.4)=0.020\,\mathrm{m}.
\end{equation}

Solving numerically gives $C\approx0.98181035$. Hence, the
modified inner profile is

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
y_{i,c}(x)=
0.02181035u^3-0.52362070u^2+0.98181035u
}
\end{equation}

where $u=x/1.5$.

The wall thickness is defined as

\begin{equation}
t(x)=y_o(x)-y_{i,c}(x).
\end{equation}
Numerical checking confirms $t(x)\ge0.020$\,m for $0.4\le x\le1.5$\,m, with
equality at $x=0.4$\,m and at the join. For both designs the wall tapers to
zero at the tip, so the minimum thickness is claimed only over this interval;
for example, the modified wall is about $0.014$\,m at $x=0.2$\,m.

\subsubsection{Internal and Material Volume}

Since the fairing is a hollow surface of revolution, its
internal volume is calculated using

\begin{equation}
V_{\mathrm{internal}}
=\pi\int_0^L y_i(x)^2\,dx.
\end{equation}

For the two designs,

\begin{align}
V_{\mathrm{internal},b}
&=\pi\int_0^{1.5}(0.9y_o(x))^2\,dx
\approx0.524807\,\mathrm{m^3},\\
V_{\mathrm{internal},c}
&=\pi\int_0^{1.5}y_{i,c}(x)^2\,dx
\approx0.584013\,\mathrm{m^3}.
\end{align}

The material occupies the region between the outer and inner
profiles. Therefore,

\begin{equation}
V_{\mathrm{material}}
=\pi\int_0^L
\left[y_o(x)^2-y_i(x)^2\right]dx.
\end{equation}

Numerical integration gives

\begin{align}
V_{\mathrm{material},b}
&\approx0.123103\,\mathrm{m^3},\\
V_{\mathrm{material},c}
&\approx0.063897\,\mathrm{m^3}.
\end{align}

Thus, the modified design provides a larger internal volume
while requiring less material.

\subsubsection{Fairing Mass and Design Comparison}

Assuming aluminium 6061 with density
$\rho_m=2700\,\mathrm{kg/m^3}$, the estimated material mass is

\begin{equation}
M=\rho_m V_{\mathrm{material}}.
\end{equation}

Therefore,

\begin{align}
M_b&=2700(0.123103)\approx332.38\,\mathrm{kg},\\
M_c&=2700(0.063897)\approx172.52\,\mathrm{kg}.
\end{align}

The reduction in estimated material mass is

\begin{align}
\Delta M&=M_b-M_c\\
&=332.38-172.52\\
&=159.86\,\mathrm{kg}.
\end{align}

The percentage reduction is

\begin{equation}
\frac{M_b-M_c}{M_b}\times100
\approx48.09\%.
\end{equation}

