% ==================================================
% 7. DISCUSSION
% ==================================================

\section{\textcolor{plakshamagenta}{Discussion}}


The results demonstrate how changing the internal geometry of a
rocket nose fairing can influence its estimated material requirements
and the behaviour predicted by a simplified powered-ascent model.
The comparison also illustrates how mathematical modelling,
integration and numerical methods can be used together to study an
engineering design problem.

\subsection{Interpretation of the Fairing Results}

The most significant result is the reduction in estimated fairing
material mass from $332.38\ \mathrm{kg}$ to
$172.52\ \mathrm{kg}$. This corresponds to a reduction of
approximately $48.09\%$.

The reduction occurs because the modified cubic inner profile
encloses a larger internal volume while the external tangent-ogive
profile remains unchanged. Since the material occupies the region
between the inner and outer profiles, increasing the internal volume
reduces the volume of material required.

The modified design also provides an internal volume of
$0.584013\ \mathrm{m^3}$, compared with
$0.524807\ \mathrm{m^3}$ for the baseline design. Therefore, the
modified profile satisfies the requirement of maintaining at least
the baseline internal volume.

The minimum wall thickness of the modified design is
$0.020\ \mathrm{m}$ over $0.4\le x\le1.5$,m. This shows that
the geometric constraint used in the mathematical formulation is
satisfied. However, satisfying a geometric thickness condition does
not establish that the fairing can withstand the mechanical and
thermal loads experienced during launch.

The mass reduction should consequently be interpreted as a theoretical
material-saving estimate rather than a confirmed reduction in the
mass of a flight-ready structure.

\subsection{Importance of the Mathematical Concepts}

Several mathematical concepts from Advanced Calculus were important
in developing the model.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Surface modelling:}
    The nose fairing was represented as a surface of revolution.
    The parameterisation
    \[
    \mathbf{X}(x,\theta)=
    \left(x,y(x)\cos\theta,y(x)\sin\theta\right)
    \]
    allowed a two-dimensional profile to describe a three-dimensional
    axisymmetric structure.

    \item \textbf{Continuity and differentiability:}
The ogive meets the body with matching height and slope ($C^1$) but not
curvature ($C^2$ fails, with a jump of $1/\rho=0.400\ \mathrm{m^{-1}}$).

    \item \textbf{Definite integration:}
    The volume of revolution formula was used to calculate the
    internal volume and material volume. These integrals connected
    the mathematical profiles directly to physical quantities
    such as volume and estimated mass.

    \item \textbf{Ordinary differential equations:}
    The rocket's vertical motion was described through a coupled
    system of differential equations for altitude, velocity and
    mass. These equations account for thrust, gravity, drag and
    propellant consumption.

    \item \textbf{Numerical methods:}
    Euler's method was used to approximate the solution of the
    coupled differential equations at successive time steps.
    This made it possible to estimate the rocket's motion when
    the governing equations could not be solved conveniently
    in closed form.
\end{itemize}

Together, these concepts demonstrate how calculus can be used to
describe geometry, calculate physical quantities and model the
time-dependent behaviour of a system.

\subsection{Analytical and Numerical Results}

The geometric part of the project begins with analytical
representations of the outer and inner fairing profiles. Their
derivatives are obtained from the defining functions, and the
volume expressions are formulated using definite integrals.

The resulting volume integrals are evaluated numerically to obtain
the reported internal and material volumes. Therefore, the
geometric results combine analytical modelling with numerical
integration.

The ascent model is different. Its governing equations form a
coupled system in which acceleration depends on changing mass,
altitude, velocity, atmospheric density and gravitational
acceleration. Since these quantities change together during flight,
Euler's method is used to approximate their evolution over time.

The numerical ascent results indicate that the modified design,
which has a lower estimated fairing mass, reaches a higher
modelled burnout velocity and altitude under the selected
assumptions. This is consistent with the general expectation
that reducing vehicle mass can improve acceleration when the
propulsion conditions are unchanged.
The numerical results depend on the time step ($\Delta t=0.01$\,s, not varied
here) and on the gravity, atmosphere and drag models, so they are estimates
rather than exact predictions.

\subsection{Assumptions Influencing the Results}

The results depend on several assumptions made during the
mathematical formulation.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Fairing geometry:}
    The fairing is assumed to be axisymmetric and is represented
    by ideal mathematical profiles. Manufacturing details,
    joints, fasteners and local structural reinforcements are
    not included.

    \item \textbf{Material properties:}
    Aluminium 6061 is assumed to have a density of
    $2700\ \mathrm{kg/m^3}$. The calculation assumes that
    the material volume is uniform and does not account for
    additional structural components.

    \item \textbf{Wall thickness:}
    The minimum wall thickness is treated as a geometric
    constraint. It is not derived from a structural stress,
    buckling or thermal analysis.

    \item \textbf{Propellant properties:}
    The LOX and RP-1 densities, mixture ratio and reference
    specific impulse are adopted from a published design-study
    reference. Actual values depend on operating conditions
    and temperature.

    \item \textbf{Vehicle parameters:}
    The total propellant mass of $4330\ \mathrm{kg}$,
    dry mass of $900\ \mathrm{kg}$ and thrust of
    $100\ \mathrm{kN}$ are preliminary design assumptions.
    They do not describe a selected or validated rocket engine
    and vehicle.

    \item \textbf{Engine performance:}
    Thrust and specific impulse are assumed constant throughout
    the powered-ascent simulation. Actual engine performance
    may vary during operation.

    \item \textbf{Aerodynamic model:}
    The drag coefficient is assumed constant at $C_D=0.3$.
    In reality, the drag coefficient varies with Mach number,
    Reynolds number, vehicle configuration and flow conditions.

\item \textbf{Atmospheric model:}
Atmospheric density is calculated using the U.S. Standard
Atmosphere 1976 model up to the specified altitude limit.
Above this limit, density is set to zero in the simulation.
This simplification affects the predicted drag and makes
the high-altitude results less reliable.

\end{itemize}

These assumptions simplify the mathematical model and make the
calculations manageable, but they also influence the accuracy
and interpretation of the results.

\subsection{Limitations of the Analysis}

Although the model provides a useful mathematical comparison,
several limitations should be considered.

First, the fairing mass calculation considers only the volume
between the idealised inner and outer profiles. It does not
include stiffeners, joints, attachment mechanisms, thermal
protection or manufacturing allowances. The estimated mass
therefore does not represent a complete fairing assembly.

Second, the minimum wall-thickness condition alone cannot
establish structural safety. A practical design would require
stress analysis, buckling analysis, vibration assessment and
evaluation of aerodynamic and thermal loading.

Third, the propulsion model uses preliminary assumptions for
thrust, dry mass and specific impulse. No specific engine has
been selected, and a complete vehicle mass budget has not been
established. The ideal rocket equation also excludes gravity
and aerodynamic losses.

Finally, the ascent simulation uses the U.S. Standard
Atmosphere 1976 model up to 84.852 km geopotential
altitude, above which atmospheric density is set to zero.
This neglects aerodynamic drag at higher altitudes.
The simulation also assumes a constant drag coefficient,
although actual aerodynamic behaviour varies with flight
conditions. The numerical burnout altitude and velocity
should therefore not be treated as validated predictions
of actual rocket performance.

Further work should extend the atmospheric model to
higher altitudes, use a more realistic aerodynamic
coefficient, incorporate a validated engine model and
include a complete structural design. These improvements
would allow a more reliable assessment of the modified
fairing and its effect on vehicle performance.


