% 8. CONCLUSION
% ==================================================

\section{\textcolor{plakshamagenta}{Conclusion}}


This project compared two rocket nose fairing designs
with a fixed external tangent-ogive profile and different
internal geometries. The baseline design used an inner
radius equal to $90\%$ of the outer radius, while the
modified design used a constrained cubic polynomial.

Using definite integration, the material volume was
estimated as $0.123103\,\mathrm{m^3}$ for the baseline
design and $0.063897\,\mathrm{m^3}$ for the modified
design. At an assumed aluminium 6061 density of
$2700\,\mathrm{kg/m^3}$, the estimated mass decreased
from $332.38\,\mathrm{kg}$ to $172.52\,\mathrm{kg}$,
representing a theoretical reduction of approximately
$48.09\%$.

The modified profile also provided a larger internal
volume while satisfying the specified wall-thickness
constraint over the evaluated interval.

A simplified powered-ascent model was developed using
ordinary differential equations and Euler's method.
Its outputs illustrate the effect of the estimated mass
change under the assumed conditions, but are not validated
flight predictions.

Overall, the project applied surface modelling,
differentiation, definite integration and numerical
methods to compare two geometric designs. Further
structural and aerodynamic analysis would be required
before either design could be considered flight-ready.

