\section{\textcolor{plakshamagenta}{Group Reflection}}

This project provided an opportunity to connect the concepts studied in Advanced Calculus with a practical engineering problem. Modelling a rocket nose fairing involved translating a physical structure into mathematical functions and using them to study its geometric properties.

One of the main learning outcomes was understanding how surface modelling, differentiation and integration can be used to analyse a three-dimensional structure and calculate meaningful physical quantities such as surface area and volume.

The project also introduced us to the role of ordinary differential equations in modelling dynamic systems. Developing a rocket ascent model and implementing Euler's method provided practical experience in approximating solutions to coupled differential equations.

Another important takeaway was recognising the role of assumptions in mathematical modelling. Simplifying a complex physical system makes calculations possible, but the results must be interpreted within the limitations of those assumptions. This became particularly evident when analysing the propulsion and ascent model.

Overall, the project strengthened our understanding of how analytical and numerical methods from Advanced Calculus can be applied to engineering problems. In future work, we would aim to incorporate more realistic physical parameters, validate the model against reliable engineering data and consider additional structural and aerodynamic constraints.

